+**Pitfalls:**
+- Camera inside volume: $t_{near}$ can be negative. Clamp $t_{range}.x$ to $\max(t_{range}.x, 0.0)$ [S2, unofficial].
+- Numerical bounds overflow: Sample UVW may slightly exceed $[0,1]$. Explicit bounds check breaks the loop [S2, unofficial].
+- Descriptor set race condition: Use two descriptor sets per slot and swap them to avoid rewriting under a frame in flight [S2, unofficial].
+- Depth sampler: Use `NEAREST` filtering and `CLAMP_TO_EDGE` for consistent occlusion sampling [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
+### [Fire Rendering](3098-fire-rendering.md)
+Fire rendering is a direct volume ray casting technique that renders fire by accumulating values determined by a reaction coordinate $Y$ rather than smoke density, using an artist-defined 1D texture to map these coordinates to fire colors. Unlike smoke rendering, which blends values during the marching process, fire rendering accumulates these reaction-based values to simulate the emissive nature of flames.
+
 ### Ember Effect
-Distinct spurts of burning particles caused by incompletely combusted fuel advected into the air [S1, unofficial]. It is integrated by simulating an additional Voxel space of ember particles [S1, unofficial]. Randomly generate ember particles at the bottom of the fire with Perlin noise [S1, unofficial]. Propel particles upwards through advection [S1, unofficial]. Raymarching procedure detects these particles and adds a small glowing aura around them to make them appear realistic [S1, unofficial].
-
-### Fire Rendering
-Rendering fire by accumulating values determined by the reaction coordinate Y rather than smoke density, using an artist-defined 1D texture that maps reaction coordinates to colors to give the appearance of fire [S2, unofficial]. March through the volume and accumulate values determined by the reaction coordinate Y, using the 1D texture [S2, unofficial]. To use the fire volume as a light source, sample the volume at several locations and treat each sample as a point light source, using the reaction coordinate and 1D color texture to determine intensity and color [S2, unofficial]. Alternative lighting approach: downsample the texture of reaction coordinates to an extremely low resolution and use every voxel as a light source [S2, unofficial]. Unlike smoke rendering which blends values during marching, fire rendering accumulates values based on the reaction coordinate [S2, unofficial]. The low-resolution voxel lighting approach is less prone to flickering but does not capture high-frequency lighting effects such as local lighting due to sparks [S2, unofficial].
+Ember effect consists of distinct spurts of burning particles caused by incompletely combusted fuel advected into the air [S1, unofficial]. It is integrated by simulating an additional Voxel space of ember particles that are randomly generated at the bottom of the fire with Perlin noise and propelled upwards through advection [S1, unofficial]. The raymarching procedure detects these particles and adds a small glowing aura around them [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Performance:** Real-time (60+ fps) on an NVIDIA RTX 2060 graphics card [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Complexity:** Linear with respect to the number of steps along the ray within the volume [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** GPU-friendly, real-time performance, realistic appearance with ember effects [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Severe flickering if treating volume samples as point light sources without enough samples [S2, unofficial]. May not capture high-frequency lighting effects with low-resolution voxel lighting [S2, unofficial]. Debugging is difficult due to HLSL limitations [S1, unofficial].
+- **Time Complexity:** Compute cost is $O(\text{pool\_size})$ regardless of active fire count, due to the fixed simulation slot pool [S2, unofficial]. Raymarch cost is $O(\text{MAX\_STEPS})$ per pixel per fire [S2, unofficial].
+- **Memory Complexity:** Fixed pool of 3D volume textures. `SIM_POOL_SIZE` is 5, meaning only 5 concurrent simulation states are maintained in GPU memory [S2, unofficial].
+- **Performance:** Achieved real-time (60+ fps) on an NVIDIA RTX 2060 with a voxel grid size of $128 \times 128 \times 128$ [S1, unofficial].
+- **Strengths:** Decouples simulation from rendering; multiple fires share simulation slots via least-used allocation, reducing memory and dispatch overhead compared to per-fire simulation [S2, unofficial].
+- **Limitations:** Fixed step size may require many steps for high accuracy; occlusion culling relies on depth buffer precision [S2, unofficial].
+- **Usage:** Used in the `migaelstrydom/VoxelPhase` project for transparent pass rendering, executed after water and before particles [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Originated from a project by CS184 students, implementing a fire simulation and rendering pipeline [S1, unofficial]. The rendering technique borrows from existing literature on volumetric clouds [S1, unofficial]. The fire rendering approach is similar to smoke rendering but uses reaction coordinate accumulation [S2, unofficial]. A more physically based discussion of fire rendering can be found in Nguyen et al. 2002 [S2, unofficial].
+This method is implemented in the `migaelstrydom/VoxelPhase` project, which uses Vulkan and Taichi for simulation and rendering [S2, unofficial]. It builds upon earlier work on fire simulation and rendering described in the CS184 Final Report, which introduced the use of reaction coordinates and ember effects [S1, unofficial]. The technique addresses the need for real-time, visually plausible fire rendering in voxel-based environments by optimizing the simulation resource usage through a shared slot pool [S2, unofficial].
 
